<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kopperunchinga I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1216–<a href="/years/1242">1242</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kopperunchinga I was a chief of the Kadava family who ruled in the northern Tamil country in the early 13th century, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> power declined. Around 1231 he captured the Chola king Rajaraja III and held him at Sendamangalam, until the intervention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king Narasimha II forced his release.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kopperunchinga-i.jpg" alt="Kopperunchinga I" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arimakhan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kopperunchinga_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kopperunchinga, also known as Jiya-Mahipati, Alagiyasiyan and Manavalapperumal, served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king Kulothunga Chola III as a subordinate between 1191 and 1195 and was related to the Chola house by marriage. Late in Kulothunga's reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king Maravarman Sundara Pandya defeated his son Rajaraja III and made the Cholas subordinate to Pandya rule. Kopperunchinga, an officer of the Chola government until about 1213, used the confusion to strengthen his own position and turned the town of Sendamangalam, in the present Kallakurichi district, into a military stronghold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His ambition brought him into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhana-ii">Singhana II</a>, whom he fought at Uratti in 1222 or 1223, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king Vira Narasimha II, who defeated him in 1224 and took the titles Establisher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> country and Destroyer of the demon Kadavaraya. Kopperunchinga recovered and defeated Rajaraja III at the battle of Tellaru, imprisoning the king and his ministers at Sendamangalam; a Chola inscription says the Lanka king Parakrama Bahu II helped him. He then styled himself Sakalabhuvanachakravartin, Emperor of the Universe.</p><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king prepared to besiege Sendamangalam, Kopperunchinga met the Hoysala armies at Perumbalur near Tiruchi in 1241, killed the generals Kesava and Harihara-Dandanayaka and others, and seized their women and property. To guard against further attacks he built a fort at Tiruvenkadu on the banks of the Kaveri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kopperunchinga died in 1242, leaving his kingdom in a strong position. He was a patron of Tamil literature and a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nataraja">Nataraja</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chidambaram">Chidambaram</a>, where he built the southern and eastern gopuras of the temple, and he improved the temples at Vennainallur and Vriddhachalam, giving the deity at Tiruvennainallur a necklace of 2,088 gems. He is also credited with laying roads, opening schools, building tanks and canals, and raising a library of several storeys on the banks of the Pennai river.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kopperunchinga I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Narasimha II intervened repeatedly to protect the weakened Chola king Rajaraja III against the Pandyas and the Kadava chief <b>Kopperunchinga</b>, and his son Vira Someshvara built a second capital at Kannanur Kuppam near Srirangam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Pallava descendants survived as minor chiefs, and in the thirteenth century the Kadava chief <b>Kopperunjinga</b>, who claimed Pallava ancestry, briefly challenged the Cholas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
